<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/advanced/organic-chemistry-functional-groups">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Ten million known organic compounds would be unlearnable one by one — so chemistry
    learned to read them by <strong>functional groups</strong>: small clusters of atoms
    that behave the same wherever they appear, because the group's own electrons, not the
    carbon skeleton, dominate its local chemistry. An –OH behaves like an alcohol on any
    skeleton; a –COOH makes any molecule an acid. Learn the twenty or so groups and the
    reaction patterns they carry, and you can predict behaviour across the whole subject —
    which is exactly the trick organic chemistry, and every biochemistry course built on
    it, is organised around. The sister concept is the <strong>homologous
    series</strong>: a family differing only by –CH₂– steps (methane, ethane, propane…),
    whose members share the group's chemistry while physical properties drift smoothly —
    boiling points rise roughly 20–30 °C per added carbon as the intermolecular grip
    grows with molecule size.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second working tool is <strong>nomenclature</strong> (syllabus 11.2): a name that
    encodes the structure. The stem counts carbons (meth-, eth-, prop-, but-), the suffix
    names the family (-ane alkane, -ene alkene, -ol alcohol, -oic acid carboxylic acid),
    and numbers locate branches and groups — counting from the end that gives the lowest
    locants. So CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₃ is propan-2-ol, and (CH₃)₃C–OH is 2-methylpropan-2-ol.
    Read any structural formula aloud with these rules and you can draw it; hear any name
    and you can build it. Chemistry's names are executable code.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four groups, four personalities — each stamped onto any carbon skeleton you like" widget="equationStep" config={funcCfg} />

  <h2>Alkanes and fuels: the saturated baseline (11.4)</h2>
  <p>
    Alkanes are the <strong>saturated</strong> baseline — every carbon spends its four
    bonds, no double bonds anywhere, so they are unreactive toward most reagents and do
    their chemistry with oxygen: burning. <strong>Complete combustion</strong> of methane:
    CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O, releasing about 890 kJ/mol. <strong>Incomplete combustion</strong>
    — limited oxygen — yields the killer intermediates: 2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O, and
    carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin roughly 200× more strongly than oxygen, which is why
    a faulty boiler kills silently. Fossil fuels are ancient alkane (and arene) mixtures:
    <strong>fractional distillation</strong> separates crude oil by boiling point into
    refinery fractions (refinery gas, petrol, naphtha, kerosene, diesel, bitumen), and
    <strong>cracking</strong> converts the over-supplied heavy fractions into the
    under-supplied light ones — vaporised long alkanes passed over a hot catalyst (or
    steam) snap into short alkanes plus <em>alkenes</em>: C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄. Cracking
    is the hinge of the whole petrochemical economy: it is where the feedstock for
    plastics comes from.
  </p>

  <h2>Alkenes: the double bond as a loaded spring (11.5)</h2>
  <p>
    The C=C double bond is a π bond — electron-rich, exposed above and below the axis,
    and therefore hungry for electrophiles. <strong>Addition reactions</strong> open the
    bond and clip new atoms on, one family per reagent: hydrogen + nickel catalyst
    (hydrogenation — margarine from vegetable oil); bromine (decolourising orange bromine
    water — the standard test that distinguishes an alkene instantly); water + acid
    catalyst (hydration — ethene + steam makes ethanol industrially, 300 °C, 60 atm,
    phosphoric acid). Because the bond is reactive in exactly one useful way, alkenes are
    the chemical industry's favourite handles. And <strong>addition polymerisation</strong>
    is just the same reaction run in a chain: n CH₂=CH₂ → –(CH₂–CH₂)ₙ– — poly(ethene),
    with the π electrons stitching monomer after monomer into the plastic bag in your
    kitchen. Every alkene monomer gives its polymer: propene → polypropene,
    chloroethene → PVC, tetrafluoroethene → PTFE. The polymer lesson takes the mechanics
    further; the group-level point is that a double bond is a chemical clutch, engaged by
    any electrophile.
  </p>

  <h2>Alcohols: hydrogen bonding and the oxidation ladder (11.6)</h2>
  <p>
    The –OH group both donates and accepts hydrogen bonds, which is why alcohols boil so
    high relative to their alkanes (ethane −89 °C, ethanol +78 °C) and why the small ones
    mix with water completely. Classify by the carbon carrying the group —
    <strong>primary</strong> (one carbon neighbour), <strong>secondary</strong> (two),
    <strong>tertiary</strong> (three) — because the oxidation chemistry depends on it,
    exactly as the worked example shows:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the ethanol-to-vinegar ladder, plus the ester finish — oxidation level is a structural property you can read off" widget="equationStep" config={ladderCfg} />

  <p>
    The industrial route to ethanol skips the ladder entirely: ethene + steam (the
    hydration above), cheap and fast; the biological route ferments glucose —
    C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂ — with yeast's enzymes, slower but renewable, and it is the
    same reaction that makes bread rise and beer beer. Ethanol is the meeting point of
    the two chemistries: one molecule, two civilisations' worth of manufacturing.
  </p>

  <h2>Carboxylic acids and esters: the acid that stays polite (11.7)</h2>
  <p>
    The –COOH group is an acid whose strength is moderated by resonance: the negative
    charge left when H⁺ departs is delocalised over two oxygens, stabilising the ion.
    Ethanoic acid has Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵, so a 0.1 mol/L solution has
    [H⁺] ≈ √(1.8 × 10⁻⁶) ≈ 1.3 × 10⁻³ mol/L and pH ≈ 2.9 — a genuine acid, but with
    barely 1% of its molecules ionised, against hydrochloric acid's essentially complete
    ionisation at the same concentration. That
    moderation is why you can put vinegar on chips. Fatty acids are long-chain versions:
    stearic acid, C₁₇H₃₅COOH, has a water-hating tail and an acid head, and that
    amphiphilic shape is what detergents exploit. The acid's partner reaction is
    <strong>esterification</strong> (above): acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + water, catalysed by
    acid, equilibrium-locked. Reverse it with alkali and you get
    <strong>saponification</strong> — fat (a tri-ester of glycerol) plus NaOH gives
    glycerol plus the sodium salts of the fatty acids: soap. One equilibrium, run both
    ways, with fragrance on one side and cleanliness on the other.
  </p>

  <h2>Condensation polymers: building with a by-product (11.8)</h2>
  <p>
    Alkenes polymerise by addition; a second great family builds by
    <strong>condensation</strong> — two different functional groups meet, join, and kick
    out a small molecule (usually water). A dicarboxylic acid plus a diol repeat the
    esterification into a <strong>polyester</strong> (PET, the drinks bottle); a
    dicarboxylic acid plus a diamine repeat the amide bond into a <strong>polyamide</strong>
    (nylon; Kevlar; and — the point the amide widget makes — proteins, where living
    chemistry runs exactly this condensation between amino acids on the ribosome).
    The practical difference is control: addition polymers need pure monomers and give
    all-or-nothing chains, while condensation lets you tune chain length with reagent
    ratios, and the by-product has to be removed or the equilibrium stops — which is why
    nylon factories spend their energy pulling water out of the melt. Both routes share
    one design rule: monomers must be at least bifunctional, or the chain cannot
    continue.
  </p>

  <h2>Chirality: chemistry's handedness</h2>
  <p>
    A carbon carrying four <em>different</em> groups comes in two non-superimposable
    mirror images — <strong>enantiomers</strong>, like left and right hands. Their
    chemistry is identical toward symmetric partners but different toward other
    <em>chiral</em> things — enzymes, receptors, DNA — because those are also
    single-handed. Consequences: the two forms rotate plane-polarised light in opposite
    directions (the historic way they were told apart), and biology tastes the
    difference (one enantiomer of carvone smells of caraway, the other of spearmint).
    The thalidomide tragedy of the late 1950s — one handedness eased morning sickness;
    the other caused severe birth defects, and the drug was sold as a mixture — is why
    every new drug today is synthesised and tested chirality by chirality. Roughly half
    of modern small-molecule drugs are sold as single enantiomers, and asymmetric
    synthesis (catalysts that build one hand selectively) earned the 2001 Nobel Prize.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "organic" means natural or healthy, and functional
    groups are arbitrary labels. Neither: organic chemistry is the chemistry of carbon
    compounds (natural and synthetic alike — petrol and paracetamol are equally organic),
    and functional groups are not labels but <em>local electronic environments</em>: the
    group's bond polarity, orbital availability and resonance pattern determine
    reactivity, which is why the same group on any skeleton behaves the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a molecule: physical trends and spot tests</h2>
  <p>
    Two practical skills round out the toolkit. First, <strong>predicting physical
    properties</strong> from intermolecular forces. Boiling needs to separate whole
    molecules, so it tracks the weakest glue between them: alkanes grip only by
    dispersion forces, which grow with chain length — butane (C₄) is a gas in a lighter,
    octane (C₈) a liquid in your engine, paraffin wax (C₂₀-plus) a solid. Branching
    lowers the grip (molecules pack worse), which is why isooctane, the petrol standard,
    boils below its straight-chain twin. Any group that hydrogen-bonds — –OH, –COOH,
    –CONH– — hoists the boiling point dramatically and often brings water-mixing with
    it: the whole ethanol-water miscibility question is hydrogen bonds, nothing more.
    Second, <strong>spot tests</strong>: a drop of bromine water decolourises in seconds
    if a C=C bond is present; Tollens' reagent (a silver mirror) distinguishes aldehydes
    from ketones by their place on the oxidation ladder; sodium hydrogencarbonate fizzing
    proves a carboxylic acid. The identification scheme in the spectroscopy lesson is
    these tests upgraded from chemical to optical.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Mechanisms: electron accounting.</strong> Every reaction above is a story of
    electron pairs moving from an electron-rich site (the alkene's π bond, the alcohol's
    oxygen, the acid's conjugate base) to an electron-poor one. Curly-arrow formalism
    makes the stories testable: nucleophile attacks electrophile, bonds break
    heterolytically, intermediates form. Two consequences worth knowing early. First,
    <strong>Markovnikov's rule</strong>: adding HX to an unsymmetrical alkene puts H on
    the carbon that already has more hydrogens, because the reaction proceeds through a
    carbocation and the more-substituted one is more stable (more alkyl neighbours
    donating electron density). Second, <strong>benzene's aromaticity</strong>: its six
    π electrons delocalise in a ring, making it far less reactive than the alkene
    analogy suggests — it substitutes rather than adds, because adding would destroy the
    stabilisation. Aromatic rings hence behave as a functional group of their own,
    the scaffold of most drugs and dyes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Resonance and acidity, quantitatively.</strong> Why is ethanoic acid a
    stronger acid than ethanol (pKa 4.76 vs 16)? The ethoxide ion left by ethanol
    concentrates −1 on one oxygen; the ethanoate ion spreads it over two — the
    conjugate base is stabilised, so the acid donates more willingly. The same logic
    ranks every acid family and explains why phenol (pKa 10) is acidic enough for soap
    manufacture while cyclohexanol is not. pKa arithmetic also runs buffers: at pH = pKa
    (Henderson–Hasselbalch), acid and conjugate base are equal — the blood's bicarbonate
    buffer sits at pH 7.4, a quarter-log unit from carbonic acid's pKa, holding pH to
    ±0.05 in the face of daily acid loads.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Retrosynthesis and the drug pipeline.</strong> Modern synthesis plans
    backwards: from the target molecule, disconnect strategic bonds to simpler precursors,
    and iterate until the tree roots in commercial chemicals. This discipline — Corey's
    Nobel-winning formalism — plus the spectroscopy of the next lesson (which tells you
    what you actually made) is the daily method of the field. The open frontier is
    selectivity at scale: making single enantiomers catalytically (organocatalysis took
    the 2021 Nobel), doing chemistry in water instead of chlorinated solvents, and
    enzymatic routes that run at room temperature — "green chemistry" as an economic
    rather than moral constraint. Meanwhile the largest synthetic challenge remains
    biological: proteins and nucleic acids are condensation polymers of exquisite
    sequence control, and the biochemistry-bridge lesson shows what happens when you let
    a cell do the synthesis.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Clayden, Greeves &amp; Warren, <em>Organic Chemistry</em>; OpenStax, <em>Organic Chemistry</em> (functional groups and nomenclature); Cambridge IGCSE Chemistry syllabus, units 11.1–11.8.</p>
</TopicPage>
